-## 1. What main does today
-
-### 1.1 priority_group (#46640)
-
-- The group is `priority_groups[min(attempt_count - 1, len - 1)]`, and the weights pick a cluster
-  inside it. The random value is computed once per request
-  (`source/extensions/router/cluster_specifiers/priority_group/priority_group_cluster_specifier.cc:288`).
-- It needs `RetryPolicy.refresh_cluster_on_retry`. `doRetry` bumps the attempt count, then calls
-  `refreshRouteCluster()`, so the specifier sees the new attempt
-  (`source/common/router/router.cc:2435-2457`).
-- Per-request groups come from `override_metadata_namespace`. The filter that writes them must
-  clear the route cache.
-- Cross-cluster retry is off while hedging is on (`router.cc:861`).
-
-### 1.2 The router's retry buffer
-
-- **Retention.** The router forwards a copy of each body frame to the live attempt
-  (`router.cc:1195`). It then keeps the frame in the HCM decoding buffer with `addDecodedData`
-  (`router.cc:1204`).
-- **Limit.** The limit is the first of these that is set:
-  1. the route's or virtual host's `request_body_buffer_limit`;
-  2. the legacy `per_request_buffer_limit_bytes`;
-  3. the stream's buffer limit (`router.cc:1097`).
-
-  When the route sets a limit, the router raises the stream's buffer limit to it
-  (`router.cc:640-646`).
-- **Overflow.** The router drops retries (`retry_or_shadow_abandoned`). If no attempt is live, the
-  request fails with a 507 (`router.cc:1147-1180`). Internal redirects are skipped too
-  (`router.cc:2247`).
-- **Replay.** Each retry copies the whole buffer into the new attempt (`router.cc:2558`).
-  `Buffer::OwnedImpl(const Instance&)` copies the bytes rather than sharing slices.
-
-### 1.3 The APM in an upstream chain
-
-- **Per-attempt chain.** Every attempt builds a new upstream filter chain from that attempt's
-  cluster (`source/common/router/upstream_request.cc:175`). The APM instance, its `BufferManager`
-  and its `ExternalBuffer` therefore last one attempt.
-- **Shared request state.** Upstream filters see the downstream `StreamInfo`
-  (`upstream_request.cc:56-60`) and the router's request header map (`upstream_request.cc:497`).
-  Header edits made during one attempt are still there on the next.
-- **In-memory tier.** The upstream filter manager takes the stream's buffer limit when the attempt
-  is created (`upstream_request.cc:173`), after the router has raised it. The APM's in-memory tier
-  is that limit (`source/extensions/filters/http/ai_protocol_manager/filter.cc:342`). With a route
-  limit set, the APM holds the whole body in heap and never writes the external buffer.
-- **Back-pressure.** Only the HCM's filter manager forwards upstream back-pressure to
-  `addUpstreamWatermarkCallbacks` subscribers (`source/common/http/conn_manager_impl.cc:2339`,
-  `:2348`). The upstream filter manager does not; that is the open follow-up from #46385. The
-  router is a downstream filter, so it does get these signals.
-- **Local replies.** A local reply from an upstream filter goes straight to the client. The retry
-  state is not consulted.
-
-### 1.4 What it costs
-
-Take a body of size B, a route limit L ≥ B, and an upstream APM on every cluster:
-
-| Copy | Where | Size | Held for |
+- **Today the APM's external buffer and router retries work against each other.**
+  - The downstream APM offloads the body.
+  - The router keeps a second, full in-memory copy for retries, bounded by the same limit.
+  - A body large enough to offload is too large to retry: the router gives up, counting
+    `retry_or_shadow_abandoned`. A body small enough to retry is held twice.
+- **The APM already keeps what a retry needs, for the whole stream.** Its store is random-access
+  and reads do not consume it. What is missing is a way for the router to read it: the APM's only
+  output injects at its own position in the chain, and anything injected after end of stream is
+  dropped.
+- **Recommendation: a verified borrowed body ("C+").**
+  - The APM publishes the body it passed on as a `Http::ReplayableRequestBody` filter-state object.
+  - The router adopts it instead of buffering, fingerprinting every byte it receives.
+  - A retry replays from the published body only after the whole body has arrived and its
+    CRC32C matches.
+  - The last chunk is held back until the replayed bytes prove equal, so even an HTTP/1.1
+    Content-Length upstream never gets a complete request built from unverified bytes.
+  - A filter that rewrites the body between the APM and the router costs the request its retry.
+    It never causes wrong bytes to be sent.
+- **Cross-domain correctness rests on two independent layers.** Either one alone is enough for
+  this pair of providers:
+  - the router restores the request headers before every cross-cluster retry;
+  - each cluster's upstream chain rebuilds its own path and credential and drops the other
+    provider's, together with a per-cluster SNI and `auto_host_rewrite`.
+- **No custom retry policy is needed** (section 5).
+- **Prototype results:**
+  - 116/116 end-to-end checks, on a real `envoy-static` (`experiment/retry-buffer-failover/`).
+  - 22/22 with header restore turned off.
+  - The existing router, APM and priority_group test targets pass, plus five new unit tests.
+
+## 1. How the APM external buffer works today
+
+### 1.1 Downstream installation
+
+```mermaid
+flowchart LR
+  C[client] -->|headers, body| A
+  subgraph stream [HCM stream]
+    A["APM<br/>holds headers<br/>BufferManager"] -->|"emitted body<br/>(injectDecodedDataToFilterChain)"| P[ext_proc] --> R[router]
+  end
+  A -->|"bytes past decoderBufferLimit"| EB[("ExternalBuffer<br/>(in_memory today)")]
+  A -->|"first bytes"| M[("pending_<br/>in-memory tier")]
+  R -->|"addDecodedData: second copy"| HB[("HCM decoding buffer")]
+```
+
+**Ingest.**
+- `decodeHeaders` holds the headers and builds one `BufferManager`, whose in-memory tier is
+  `decoderBufferLimit()` (`filter.cc:342`).
+- Every frame goes to the parser, then to `BufferManager::onData`.
+- Past the tier, the manager creates the store (`buffer_manager.cc:143-157`).
+- Writes are batched up to 64 KiB, with one write in flight at a time.
+- Bytes that are not yet durable count toward an ingest watermark. Above it, the manager calls
+  `onDecoderFilterAboveWriteBufferHighWatermark`, which read-disables the client.
+
+**Filters and emission.** At end of body the AI filters run on the parsed document. Then the APM
+emits:
+
+| `reserialize_body` | Bytes emitted | Code |
+|---|---|---|
+| `DISABLE` | `store[0, len)`, exactly as received | `request_filter_manager.cc:188` |
+| `ALWAYS` (default) | Serializer walk: inline JSON fragments (`inject`) interleaved with store ranges for large strings (`replay`) | `serializer.cc:54-113` |
+
+**How emission reaches the next filter.**
+- Every chunk goes through `DecoderFilterChainBridge::injectData`, which calls
+  `injectDecodedDataToFilterChain(data, false)`. The first chunk releases the held headers.
+- Emission is paced by upstream watermarks and capped at 8 x 64 KiB per event-loop pass.
+- An empty `end_stream` frame, or `continueDecoding()` for trailers, ends the body
+  (`filter.cc:491-500`).
+
+**Lifetime.**
+- The store and the in-memory tier are freed only in `BufferManager::onDestroy`, at stream
+  teardown.
+- Reads copy rather than consume (`buffer_manager.cc:410`, `external_buffer_impl.cc:46-53`).
+- The APM's `onDestroy` runs before the router's (`filter_manager.h:757-762`).
+
+**Why it cannot re-emit for a retry.**
+- `replay()` injects at the APM's position.
+- After the end marker, `FilterManager::decodeData` drops anything injected
+  (`filter_manager.cc:776-778`).
+- `buffer_` is private, with no passive read. The TODO at `buffer_manager.h:208` asks for one.
+
+### 1.2 Upstream installation
+
+```mermaid
+flowchart LR
+  R[router] -->|"full copy, attempt 1"| U1["upstream APM #1<br/>new BufferManager + store"] --> G[gemini]
+  R -->|"full copy, attempt 2"| U2["upstream APM #2<br/>re-parses, new store"] --> V[vertex]
+```
+
+- **New instance per attempt.** Each attempt builds a new upstream chain from that attempt's
+  cluster (`upstream_request.cc:175`). The APM, its `BufferManager` and its store live for that
+  attempt only. The router feeds each one a full copy (`router.cc:2558` on main).
+- **In-memory tier.** It is the downstream stream limit as read at attempt creation
+  (`upstream_request.cc:173`). That limit is the one the router raised, so a body the router can
+  retry never reaches the external buffer.
+- **No back-pressure.** The upstream filter manager never forwards watermarks to
+  `addUpstreamWatermarkCallbacks` subscribers. That is the #46385 follow-up.
+- **Local replies are final.** An upstream filter's local reply goes straight to the client,
+  with no retry (`upstream_request.cc:64-75`).
+- **Shared state.** Upstream filters share the downstream `StreamInfo` and the router's header map
+  (`upstream_request.cc:56-61, 497`). What one attempt writes, the next attempt sees.
+
+So the upstream APM can rewrite the body per attempt, but it cannot hold the retry body.
+
+### 1.3 What retries cost today
+
+| Body size B vs. stream limit L | Router | APM | Result |
 |---|---|---|---|
-| Router retry copy | HCM heap | B | the request |
-| Attempt copy (`router.cc:2558`), held by the APM in-memory tier | heap | B | the attempt, until the APM finishes replaying |
-
-The peak is about 2B of heap per request. A body larger than L gets no retries at all, so the
-external buffer is only ever used for bodies the router could not retry anyway.
-
-With the APM downstream instead, the APM does offload. Its tier is the unraised limit, because it
-holds the headers and the router has not run yet. But the router then buffers all of B in heap.
-
-Either way, enabling retries puts the whole body back in the HCM buffers, which is what the
-external buffer is there to avoid.
-
