-- **`num_retries`.** Set it to at least the number of groups minus one. The retry circuit breaker
-  belongs to the first attempt's cluster (`router.cc:851`) and governs every retry, so use a fixed
-  `num_retries` rather than a retry budget.
-- **Backoff.** Leave `rate_limited_retry_back_off` unset. Otherwise provider A's `Retry-After`
-  delays the move to provider B. Keep `retry_back_off` short.
-- **Buffer limit.** `request_body_buffer_limit` caps the bodies that can be retried. A larger body
-  gets a single attempt.
-- **Providers.** Without the fixes in section 5, this configuration is only safe when every
-  provider speaks the same API with the same model name.
-
-## 3. Why the upstream APM's buffer can't be the retry buffer
-
-1. **Lifetime.** The buffer is deleted together with its attempt.
-2. **Direction.** The router replays the body into the upstream chain. The APM receives that
-   replay; it does not produce it.
-3. **Gaps between attempts.** No attempt exists during backoff or async host selection, or after an
-   attempt resets mid-upload. Downstream bytes keep arriving, and the router has to keep them.
-4. **Mixed chains.** The next attempt's cluster may not run an APM at all.
-5. **Single writer.** `ExternalBuffer` allows one writer at a time. The router and every attempt's
-   APM would all be writers.
-
-Moving the APM's buffer into filter state when the filter is destroyed solves only the first
-problem. So ownership flips: the router owns the per-request copy, built from the same
-external-buffer extension, and the upstream APM borrows it.
-
-## 4. Proposal
-
-### 4.1 One external-buffer interface in core
-
-- Move `ExternalBuffer`, `ExternalBufferFactory` and `ExternalBufferConfigFactory` out of
-  `source/extensions/filters/http/ai_protocol_manager/external_buffer.h` into
-  `envoy/buffer/external_buffer.h`, under a core extension category (D4).
-- Move the in-memory implementation out of the APM. The APM is alpha, so its
-  `external_buffer_config` can take the new category directly.
-- Split `BufferManager` along the line its TODO already draws (`buffer_manager.h:208`):
-  - The passive spool moves to `source/common/buffer/`: the memory tier, single-writer batching,
-    the durable length and ranged reads.
-  - The replay driver stays with each consumer.
-
-  The router and the APM then share one spool and one set of flow-control rules.
-
-These moves change nothing for the APM.
-
-### 4.2 Router: spill the retry buffer
-
-```proto
-// envoy.extensions.filters.http.router.v3.Router
-message Router {
-  // Keeps the request body retained for retries once it outgrows the route's in-memory request
-  // body buffer limit, instead of giving up on retrying the request.
-  RetryBodySpool retry_body_spool = <next>;
-}
-
-message RetryBodySpool {
-  // [#extension-category: <D4>]
-  config.core.v3.TypedExtensionConfig external_buffer = 1
-      [(validate.rules).message = {required: true}];
-
-  // A body larger than this is not retried, as with an in-memory overflow today.
-  google.protobuf.UInt64Value max_bytes = 2 [(validate.rules).message = {required: true}];
-}
+### 4.2 Core contract: `Http::ReplayableRequestBody`
+
+`envoy/http/replayable_request_body.h` defines a `StreamInfo::FilterState::Object` under the key
+`envoy.http.replayable_request_body`:
+
+| Method | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `length()` | Bytes the publisher has passed on so far |
+| `complete()` | Every byte has been passed on, and the publisher can still serve reads |
+| `crc32c()` | CRC32C of the bytes passed on so far |
+| `read(offset, max, cb) const` | Delivers 1..max bytes from `offset`. May complete inline (the handle is then null); destroying the handle cancels the read; an error is final |
+
+Contract:
+- The publisher publishes before its first body byte, with `LifeSpan::FilterChain`, so a stream
+  recreated for an internal redirect never inherits it.
+- The router pins the object with a `shared_ptr` (`getDataSharedMutableGeneric`).
+- The contract promises what the publisher passed on, not what reached the router; the router
+  checks the two match.
+- Any filter that holds the whole body can publish one. Later candidates are a buffer filter with
+  a store, or a router-owned spool (option B).
+
+### 4.3 APM: record what was emitted, publish it
+
+With `RequestHandling.serve_router_retries` set on a downstream installation, `finalizeDecode`
+(`filter.cc:509`):
+1. calls `BufferManager::recordEmission()`;
+2. publishes an `EmittedRequestBody`, which holds a `weak_ptr<BufferManager>`;
+3. marks it complete when emission finishes.
+
+`complete()` turns false once the manager is detached, so a vanished publisher fails closed.
+
+**The record** (`buffer_manager.cc:524`) is made of spans:
+- **Store spans:** an offset and length into `pending_` or the store, with no copy.
+- **Inline spans:** copies of serializer fragments.
+
+Adjacent spans merge, and a CRC32C runs over every emitted byte. Under `DISABLE` the record is a
+single store span. Under `ALWAYS` it costs only the JSON skeleton: strings above
+`inline_string_threshold_bytes` stay in the store.
+
+**Reads.** `readEmitted()` (`buffer_manager.cc:570`) serves one span at a time:
+- Inline bytes and the in-memory tier complete on the caller's stack.
+- Store reads go through `ExternalBuffer::read`, with a liveness token so a cancelled read never
+  calls back.
+
+**For the PR:**
+- Publish from the `RequestFilterManager` sink after the counting pass. That is where the mode,
+  the size and the `ALWAYS` inline byte count are known, so the APM can skip publishing when the
+  skeleton exceeds a cap, and the router keeps today's path.
+- Factor the record into an `EmissionRecord` class.
+- Pass Content-Length as the `createBuffer` hint. The hint is never passed today.
+
+### 4.4 Router: adopt, verify, replay
+
+**Adopt** (`maybeUseReplayableBody`). The router adopts the published body when all of these
+hold:
+- retries are enabled;
+- there is no internal-redirect policy (redirects re-run the chain over the decoding buffer);
+- there is no hedging;
+- the request is not CONNECT or an upgrade;
+- the request has a body;
+- a published body exists.
+
+**Attempt 1, live** (`decodeData`).
+- Each frame extends the received CRC32C and is moved to the attempt. There is no
+  `addDecodedData` and no buffer-limit check.
+- While a retry waits or replays, frames are drained; the replay reads them back.
+- Shadows still get copies.
+
+**Verify** (`onRequestComplete`). At end of body the published body must be complete, the same
+length and the same CRC32C.
+- **Match:** the body is marked verified, and a waiting retry starts.
+- **Mismatch:**
+  - Retries are abandoned, counting `retry_body_mismatch` and `retry_or_shadow_abandoned`.
+  - Attempt 1 continues with the router's own bytes.
+  - A retry that is waiting or in backoff gets a 503.
+
+**Replay.**
+- `continueDoRetry` restores the headers and sends them with `end_stream` false whenever a body
+  is or may be coming.
+- If the downstream finished during backoff, it also arms the global timeout, which
+  `onRequestComplete` could not arm with no live attempt (a pre-existing gap).
+- A retry that starts mid-upload waits until the body is verified. No published byte goes out
+  before verification, so no attempt ever gets a prefix from the store spliced onto a different
+  live tail.
+- After verification, the replay reads `[0, len)` in 64 KiB chunks:
+  - It pauses only on its own attempt's flow control, through the new
+    `RouterFilterInterface::onUpstreamRequestBodyFlowControl` hook. The hook is called from
+    `UpstreamRequest::disable/enableDataFromDownstreamForFlowControl`. Unrelated shadows or side
+    streams do not stall it.
+  - Every resume is posted.
+  - A pass replays at most 8 chunks, then yields to the next event-loop iteration.
+- **The last chunk is held:** the replay CRC must equal the received CRC before the chunk that
+  completes the request is sent, with `end_stream` or followed by trailers. On a mismatch the
+  attempt is reset with a 503 `retry_body_replay_mismatch`.
+
+**Cancellation.**
+- Each replay has a generation number. Every read callback re-checks it and confirms that its
+  attempt is still the front one.
+- `cleanup()`, `resetAll()` and `onDestroy()` stop the replay.
+- `onUpstreamComplete` resets an attempt whose response ends before its replayed body does.
+- For the PR, the replayer moves into the `UpstreamRequest` and is destroyed first in `cleanUp()`.
+  `cleanUp()` is the single choke point every attempt passes through on its way out.
+
+```mermaid
+stateDiagram-v2
+  [*] --> Live: published body adopted
+  Live --> Abandoned: end of body, length or CRC mismatch
+  Live --> Verified: end of body, match
+  Live --> Waiting: attempt fails mid-upload, retry created
+  Waiting --> Replaying: end of body, match
+  Waiting --> Failed: end of body, mismatch (503)
+  Verified --> Replaying: retry created
+  Replaying --> Replaying: 64 KiB read, sent, paced by the attempt
+  Replaying --> Sent: replay CRC matches, last chunk + end_stream
+  Replaying --> Failed: read error or replay CRC mismatch, last chunk withheld (503)
+  Sent --> Replaying: attempt fails, next retry
 ```
 
-- **Small bodies.** A body within the in-memory limit behaves exactly as it does today.
-- **Crossing the limit.** The router moves the decoding buffer into the spool and appends every
-  later frame there. It pauses the downstream while the spool's unacknowledged bytes exceed the
-  stream limit, the same rule the APM's bridge applies.
-- **Past `max_bytes`.** The router falls back to today's overflow handling.
-- **Replaying a spilled body.**
-  - Headers go out at once.
-  - The body is read back from the spool in chunks, paced by the attempt's write watermarks.
-  - If the downstream has not finished, v1 holds the body replay until end of stream (D3). Live
-    frames go only to the spool.
-- **Spool errors.** A failed read or write ends the request with a local 500 and
-  `retry_body_spool_error`, as the APM does for its own buffer errors.
-- **Internal redirects** are off for a spilled body, as they are for an overflowed one. Mirrors are
-  unchanged, because they stream live copies.
-- **Configuration.** With a spool, operators leave `request_body_buffer_limit` unset or small. The
-  router then stops raising the stream limit to fit the largest prompt, and an upstream APM's
-  in-memory tier stays small too.
-
-### 4.3 The upstream APM borrows the router's body
-
-- **Accessor.** Add an upstream-only accessor, `Http::UpstreamStreamFilterCallbacks::retainedRequestBody()`
-  (D6). It returns a read-only view of what the router retains for this request, whether in memory
-  or spilled: length, completeness, and async ranged reads with cancellation. It returns null when
-  the router retains nothing.
-- **Retain before forward.** The router retains each frame before forwarding it. Any byte an
-  upstream filter has seen can then be read from the view. Today it is the other way round
-  (`router.cc:1195` runs before `:1204`).
-- **Borrow mode.** Add `RequestHandling.use_router_retained_body` (opt-in, D5). In an upstream
-  chain with a non-null view, the `BufferManager` feeds each frame to the parser, counts it and
-  drops it. Replay reads ranges from the view. Parser offsets stay valid because the frames are the
-  retained bytes, starting at offset 0.
-- **Integrity.** An upstream filter ahead of the APM that edits the body would break that
-  assumption. The flag documents the requirement. At end of stream, the APM fails closed with a 500
-  if the byte count it saw differs from the view's length.
-- **Later, optional.** Cache the unmodified parse per request so attempts 2..N skip re-parsing.
-  AI filters would then edit a per-attempt clone.
-
-### 4.4 Memory per request
-
-Take a body B larger than the in-memory limit, with one live attempt:
-
-| | Router | Live attempt (APM) | Retries above the limit |
-|---|---|---|---|
-| Today | heap B, only if B ≤ L | heap B | no |
-| 4.2, in-memory backend | heap B | heap B (own in-memory buffer) | yes |
-| 4.2, disk/remote backend | heap O(chunk), store B | heap O(limit), store B | yes |
-| 4.2 + 4.3, in-memory backend | heap B | heap O(frame) | yes |
-| 4.2 + 4.3, disk/remote backend | heap O(chunk), store B | heap O(frame) | yes |
-
-The in-memory implementation is the only backend in tree. So 4.2 alone makes large bodies
-retriable but saves no heap. 4.3 is what halves it.
-
-## 5. Prerequisites for cross-provider fallback
-
-These are not buffer work, but retained bodies are no use without them when the providers differ.
-
-1. **Header restore per attempt (router).** Upstream filters edit the router's header map: `:path`,
-   `content-length` and credentials. The router should snapshot the headers before the first
-   attempt and restore them at the top of `doRetry` on cross-cluster retries. This was proposed in
-   the model-failover design.
-2. **Per-attempt AI target (APM).** `envoy.ai.llm_protocol.response`, `envoy.ai.binding.response`
-   and `envoy.ai.model.resolved` are request filter state
-   (`ai_protocol_manager/ai_filter_state.h:30-41`), and the route's per-filter config is the same
-   on every attempt. Every attempt therefore gets the same backend API, binding and model.
-   Proposal: the upstream APM's own config declares its cluster's backend (`llm_protocol`,
-   `binding`, `model`), and in an upstream chain that declaration wins over the request filter
-   state (D7).
-3. **Credentials.** Run `credential_injector` per cluster with `overwrite: true`. This is safe only
-   once item 1 lands; until then attempt 1's header survives under a name attempt 2 does not
-   overwrite.
-4. **Terminal local replies.** A transcode or schema rejection at attempt k ends the request
-   without trying attempt k+1. v1 accepts this: a payload one provider rejects locally is usually
-   malformed for all of them.
-
-## 6. Do we need a custom retry policy?
-
-No:
-
-| Need | Existing mechanism |
+### 4.5 Per-attempt request adaptation
+
+**Layer 1: router header restore.**
+- When `cross_cluster_retry_` is set, the router copies the request headers before the first
+  attempt's upstream filters run. The shadow path already does the same (`router.cc:886-892`).
+- `doRetry` restores them in place before `refreshRouteCluster()`. It uses the internal-redirect
+  idiom: `clear()` followed by `RequestHeaderMapImpl::copyFrom`.
+- Attempt count, timeout headers and `auto_host_rewrite` are applied per attempt afterwards, as
+  today.
+- The restore sits behind the runtime guard
+  `envoy.reloadable_features.restore_request_headers_on_cross_cluster_retry`.
+
+**Layer 2: self-normalizing cluster chains** (`HttpProtocolOptions.http_filters`):
+- Lua rebuilds `:path` from the `/models/{m}:{method}` suffix, which covers `:generateContent`
+  and `:streamGenerateContent?alt=sse`, and removes the other provider's credential.
+  `header_mutation` cannot set pseudo-headers.
+- `credential_injector` uses the generic credential with `overwrite: true`:
+  - gemini: `header: x-goog-api-key`;
+  - vertex: `header: authorization`, `header_value_prefix: "Bearer "`, from an SDS secret that a
+    token minter refreshes.
+- `upstream_codec` comes last.
+- No Lua `respond()`: an upstream local reply is final and skips failover.
+
+Evidence for the two layers:
+- With layer 1 off and naive chains, Vertex receives Gemini's key and answers 400, and Gemini
+  receives Vertex's path and token and answers 404 (`captured/e2e_no_restore_naive_chains.log`).
+- With layer 1 off and layer 2 on, failover is clean.
+- Layer 1 is still the general fix: it makes arbitrary upstream filters safe.
+
+**Route, router and clusters:**
+- `auto_host_rewrite: true`, applied per attempt at pool-ready. `endpoint.hostname` must be set.
+- `request_headers_to_remove: [x-pdp-decision]`.
+- `suppress_envoy_headers: true`.
+- An explicit `UpstreamTlsContext.sni` per cluster.
+- Do not use `auto_sni` or `auto_san_validation` on clusters in a cross-cluster group: they are
+  computed once per request from attempt 1 (`router.cc:675-716`).
+- No cluster-level `retry_policy`: it replaces the route's and can turn cross-cluster retry off.
+- Size `circuit_breakers.max_retries` on both clusters. The first attempt's cluster's breaker,
+  default 3, gates every failover.
+- HTTP/2 upstreams are preferred. HTTP/1.1 is safe because the last chunk is held.
+
+The body is the same Gemini `GenerateContent` JSON for both clusters: both bindings remove `model`
+and `stream`, and the model lives in the path. No attempt rewrites the body. A provider pair with
+different bodies needs option D for that pair.
+
+### 4.6 PDP integration (ext_proc)
+
+**Processing mode.** Strictly header-only: request headers `SEND`, bodies `NONE`, response and
+trailers `SKIP`. Any body mode would put the offloaded body back in the HCM buffer and allow
+mutation. A violation, such as `CONTINUE_AND_REPLACE` or a body mutation, is caught by the CRC
+check.
+
+**Inputs.**
+- `request_attributes: ["filter_state['envoy.ai.model.request']"]` sends the model the APM
+  parsed. The prototype PDP sends `gemini-2.5-pro` to Vertex first.
+- Optionally, `metadata_options.forwarding_namespaces.typed: [envoy.ai.request_info]`.
+
+**Output.**
+- Untyped `envoy.ai.routing: {priority_groups: [...]}`, received through `receiving_namespaces`.
+- `route_cache_action: CLEAR`, together with a header mutation. Without the mutation, ext_proc
+  ignores the cache clear (`processor_state.cc:771-778`), and attempt 1 uses the configured order
+  while retries use the PDP's.
+- `mutation_rules` restrict the mutation to `x-pdp-*`.
+- The list of groups is indexed by attempt, and the last group repeats, so one PDP call covers
+  every attempt.
+
+**Failure.** With `failure_mode_allow`, a PDP outage falls back to the configured order. Set
+`message_timeout` explicitly; a fail-closed timeout is a hard-coded 504.
+
+### 4.7 Failure modes
+
+| Event | Behavior |
+|---|---|
+| Filter between the APM and the router changes the body | Mismatch at end of body; no retry. Attempt 1 keeps the router's own bytes; a waiting retry gets a 503 |
+| The same, with attempt 1 reset mid-upload | The retry waits for verification and fails closed; the provider gets nothing |
+| Store read fails during a replay | Attempt reset before its last chunk; 503 `retry_body_replay_error` |
+| Store returns different bytes on readback | Replay CRC mismatch; last chunk withheld; 503 |
+| Attempt reset or timed out during an asynchronous read | Generation and front checks make the callback a no-op |
+| Response completes before the replay | Attempt reset; no half-open stream |
+| APM gone first at teardown | `complete()` is false, reads fail; the router is being torn down too |
+| Stream recreated by an internal redirect | `FilterChain` lifespan: the new stream does not inherit the object |
+| Downstream finishes during backoff | Global timeout armed when the retry starts |
+| Synchronous store with watermarks off | At most 8 chunks per pass |
+| Upstream-filter local reply (credential, Lua) | Final, as today; not failed over |
+| No published body | Today's in-memory path |
+
+### 4.8 Memory per request
+
+| Store backend | Today | C+ |
+|---|---|---|
+| In memory | APM B + router B; no retry if B > L | APM B (+ inline skeleton under `ALWAYS`); retries at any size |
+| Disk or remote | Router B on the heap; no retry if B > L | Heap O(64 KiB) per replay; one durable copy |
+
+## 5. Do we need a custom retry policy?
+
+No. None of Envoy's retry extension points (`RetryPriority`, `RetryHostPredicate`,
+`RetryOptionsPredicate`) touch body retention, and every routing concern already has a home:
+
+| Need | Mechanism |
+|---|---|
+| Per-attempt cluster order | `priority_group` + `refresh_cluster_on_retry` |
+| Per-request order | PDP metadata through `override_metadata_namespace` |
+| When to retry | `retry_on: 5xx,reset,connect-failure,retriable-status-codes` + `retriable_status_codes: [429]` |
+| Keep one provider's `Retry-After` from delaying the other | Leave `rate_limited_retry_back_off` unset; short `retry_back_off` |
+| Attempt count | `num_retries` (groups minus one, unless the last group should repeat) |
+| Retain and replay the body | Router + published body |
+| Per-provider request | The cluster's upstream filters |
+
+An error-body classifier is the one remaining use, for example OpenAI's 429
+`insufficient_quota`. It can be an upstream filter that sets a response header for
+`retriable_headers`. No new retry extension is needed.
+
+## 6. Prototype
+
+**Code.** About 600 lines:
+- `envoy/http/replayable_request_body.h`;
+- `envoy/router/router_filter_interface.h`: the per-attempt flow-control hook, plus its mock;
+- APM: `buffer_manager.{h,cc}`, `filter.{h,cc}`, and the `serve_router_retries` proto field;
+- router: `router.{h,cc}`, `upstream_request.{h,cc}`, stats in `context_impl.h`, the runtime guard;
+- BUILD files;
+- five unit tests in `buffer_manager_test.cc`.
+
+**Harness** (`experiment/retry-buffer-failover/`; see its README):
+- `envoy.yaml` with three listeners: :19000 (`DISABLE`), :19004 (`ALWAYS`), and the :19002
+  control with today's buffer. All use a 32 KiB connection limit.
+- `pdp/`: a Go ext_proc PDP.
+- `mock_llm.py`: TLS mock Gemini and Vertex backends. They record SNI, host, path, credentials
+  and the body's SHA-256, and check credentials the way Google does.
+- `e2e.py` and `run.sh`.
+- A trimmed `build_config/`: a cold build of `envoy-static` takes about 7.5 minutes.
+
+**Results** (`captured/`):
+
+| Case | Result |
+|---|---|
+| Baseline; 429, reset and 503 failover; 4 MiB fixed-length and chunked | Every attempt SHA-identical to the client's body, with its own path, credential, host and SNI |
+| 64 MiB, attempt 1 reset mid-upload | Retry waits for the rest of the body, verifies, replays 64 MiB; SHA-identical |
+| PDP reverses order (by header, or `gemini-2.5-pro` by parsed model) | Gemini served second with its own path and key |
+| `ALWAYS`: 4 MiB failover, reversed order, 64 MiB mid-upload reset | Every attempt gets the same re-serialized bytes (4,194,218 vs the client's 4,194,226) with a matching `content-length` |
+| Both providers fail | Three attempts (the last group repeats); the last 503 reaches the client |
+| Lua rewrites the body between the APM and the router | `retry_body_mismatch`; Vertex never called; client gets Gemini's 429 |
+| The same with an early reset | No retry, no 200; Vertex never called |
+| Control listener, 4 MiB | Today's behavior: retry abandoned, 429 |
+| Header restore off, naive chains | Vertex 400 for two credentials; Gemini 404 for Vertex's path |
+| Header restore off, self-normalizing chains | Clean failover (22/22) |
+
+Large bodies went to the external buffer ("offloading, 65296 bytes exceeds the in-memory limit of
+32768"), and the router never raised the stream buffer limit. These targets pass:
+- `router_test`, `upstream_request_test`, `retry_state_impl_test`;
+- APM `buffer_manager_test`, `filter_test`, `filter_manager_test` and its integration test;
+- the priority_group integration test.
+
+**Not in the prototype:**
+- an attempt-owned replayer;
+- a test-only publisher and router unit and integration tests;
+- the `ALWAYS` inline cap;
+- docs and release notes.
